      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Twisting of lung lobe(s) at the hilus with occlusion or narrowing of the bronchus, lymphatics, vein, and (finally) arteries.",
              "pdf_page": 925,
              "printed_page": 843,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Affected lobes—right middle lobe most commonly affected (especially in large dogs) with preexisting pleural effusion; other lobes can twist singly or in pairs. Occasionally torsion occurs in midlobar area. Pugs are very commonly affected, with the left cranial",
              "pdf_page": 925,
              "printed_page": 843,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "lobe most common.",
              "pdf_page": 925,
              "printed_page": 843,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Initially, the lobe becomes engorged with blood and enlarges; infarction and necrosis may follow; hemorrhagic pleural effusion typically develops.",
              "pdf_page": 925,
              "printed_page": 843,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
